    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d614">

                <pb facs="0679" n="575" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg575"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/4711: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-03-12T10:00:00+08:00">March 12, 1932—10
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 12—5:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My March 11, 2 p.m. Following is Quo Tai-chi’s reply dated March 11th to the
                    Japanese Minister’s letter.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“With reference to Mr. Shigemitsu’s letter to you of yesterday, March 10, a
                        copy [of] which you were good enough to hand me this noon, I wish to state
                        that the expression ‘complete withdrawal’ in our reply yesterday implies
                        withdrawal of the Japanese forces and [<hi rendition="#italic">which?</hi>]
                        revert to the position prior to their advance on the night of January
                        28.”</p></quote>
                <p>In communicating a copy of the above to the Japanese Minister, Sir Miles <pb
                        facs="0680" n="576" xml:id="pg576"/> said that he assumed Shigemitsu would
                    let him know when he feels that the time has arrived for a meeting with the
                    Chinese.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
